Velocity, (u) $=60 \frac{\mathrm{km}}{\mathrm{h}}=\frac{60 \times 1000}{60 \times 60} \frac{\mathrm{m}}{\mathrm{s}}$ Height, (h) = $490 \mathrm{~m}\begin{aligned}
& S=u t \\
& =\sqrt[u]{\frac{2 h}{g}}\left(\because h=u t+\frac{1}{2} g t^2\right) \\
& =\frac{60 \times 1000}{60 \times 60} \times \frac{\sqrt{2 \times 490}}{9.8}=\frac{500}{3} \mathrm{~m}
\end{aligned}$

Angular speed, $\omega=2 \omega\begin{aligned}
& \frac{a_1}{a_2}=\frac{R \omega_1^2}{R \omega_2^2} \\
& \Rightarrow \frac{a_1}{a_2}=\frac{R \omega^2}{R(2 \omega)^2}
\end{aligned}\begin{aligned} & =\frac{\mathrm{R} \omega^2}{4 \mathrm{R} \omega^2}=\frac{1}{4} \\ & \because \mathrm{a}_2=4 \mathrm{a}_1\end{aligned}$

Energy equivalent of $1 \mathrm{~kg}$ of mass, $\mathrm{E}=\mathrm{mc}^2\begin{aligned}
& =1 \times\left(3 \times 10^8\right)^2 \\
& =9 \times 10^{16} \mathrm{~J}
\end{aligned}$

Angular momentum will remain conserved
$\begin{aligned}
& \mathrm{L}=\mathrm{I} \omega \\
& \therefore \mathrm{I}_1 \omega_1=\mathrm{I}_2 \omega_2 \\
& \Rightarrow \frac{2}{5} \mathrm{MR}^2 \times \frac{2 \pi}{\mathrm{T}_1}=\frac{2}{5} \mathrm{M}(\mathrm{xR})^2 \times \frac{2 \pi}{\mathrm{T}_2}
\end{aligned}\begin{aligned} & \Rightarrow T_2=T_1 x^2 \\ & \Rightarrow T_2=24 x^2\end{aligned}$

Spring constant, $\mathrm{k}=\frac{\mathrm{F}}{\mathrm{x}}$, unit of spring constant $=\frac{\mathrm{N}}{\mathrm{m}}\begin{aligned}
& \text { surface energy }=\frac{\text { Energy }}{\text { Area }} \\
& =\frac{\text { Joule }}{\mathrm{m}^2}=\frac{\mathrm{Nm}}{\mathrm{m}^2}=\frac{\mathrm{N}}{\mathrm{m}}
\end{aligned}$

Masses, $\mathrm{m}_1=1 \mathrm{~g} ; \mathrm{m}_2=4 \mathrm{~g}$ Kinetic energy, $K E=\frac{p^2}{2 m}\begin{aligned}
& \frac{\mathrm{P}_1^2}{2 \mathrm{~m}_1}=\frac{\mathrm{P}_2^2}{2 \mathrm{~m}_2} \\
& \Rightarrow \frac{\mathrm{P}_1^2}{\mathrm{p}_2^2}=\frac{\mathrm{m}_1}{\mathrm{~m}_2} \\
& \frac{\mathrm{p}_1}{\mathrm{p}_2}=\sqrt{\frac{\mathrm{m}_1}{\mathrm{~m}_2}}=\sqrt{\frac{1}{4}}=\frac{1}{2}
\end{aligned}$
